<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1105 CE – 1427 CE (approx.) · capital Cadolzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The castle office at Nuremberg that gave the Hohenzollerns their first foothold in Franconia, the start of their rise to kings of Prussia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1105 the Salian emperors appointed burgraves to command the royal castle of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. The first family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-raabs">Raabs</a>, died out around 1190, and Frederick of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">Zollern</a>, a Swabian noble married to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raabs</a>' heiress, became burgrave in 1192, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Franconian Hohenzollern</a> line.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The burgraves held the castle, rights of justice and lands around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, and gradually built a territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, acquiring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-ansbach">Ansbach</a> in 1331 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-nuremberg">Frederick III of Nuremberg</a> helped elect Rudolf I in 1273. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> itself grew into a free imperial city and rival.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1415 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-brandenburg">Burgrave Frederick VI</a> received the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a> as Elector Frederick I. In 1427 he sold the burgrave's castle to the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. His Franconian lands became the margraviates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Brandenburg-Kulmbach</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/burgraviate-of-nuremberg.jpg" alt="Burgraviate of Nuremberg" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: --TECHNOKRAT (talk) 14:55, 2 October 2008 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgraves_of_Nuremberg_(Eigener_Entwurf_Version_2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1105 CE – 1427 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cadolzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick I of Nuremberg, Frederick III of Nuremberg, Frederick VI of Nuremberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Burggrafschaft Nürnberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=burgraviate-of-nuremberg" aria-label="Burgraviate of Nuremberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 454 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Burgraviate of Nuremberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5250 710L5229 694M5250 710L5244 715M5250 710L5265 696"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5244 715h0M5265 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cadolzburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1266&amp;focus=burgraviate-of-nuremberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
